#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Background {#background}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Many machine learning problems in industry involve small- or medium-sized "
"tabular datasets, where adding more data is not an option and model accuracy"
" plateaus. Credit risk scoring is a typical example. A lender uses a few "
"dozen attributes to decide whether the applicant is likely to repay the "
"credit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Rimay, a Qiskit Function by Kipu Quantum, adds a quantum preprocessing step "
"to such a pipeline. It implements the digitized counterdiabatic quantum "
"feature extraction algorithm [\\[1\\]](#references). Every input feature is "
"mapped to one qubit. The feature values and the mutual information between "
"features set the local fields and couplings of a spin-glass Hamiltonian. "
"Counterdiabatic dynamics then evolve the quantum system toward its low-"
"energy states, and the single- and two-body expectation values measured at "
"the end become the new, quantum-transformed features. These features capture"
" nonlinear correlations between the inputs, and you can feed them into any "
"classical model.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Pipeline with Rimay inserted between the train-test split and the "
"classical machine learning model](/docs/images/tutorials/classify-credit-"
"risk-with-rimay-quantum-feature-extraction/pipeline.svg \"Rimay as an "
"additional preprocessing step in a classical machine learning pipeline\")\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This tutorial uses the German credit dataset from OpenML "
"[\\[2\\]](#references), with 1000 applicants and 20 attributes, to train a "
"random forest classifier. You first establish a classical baseline, then "
"send the data to Rimay, and finally train the same kind of model on the "
"quantum-transformed features, on their own and combined with the original "
"features.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Requirements {#requirements}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Before you start this tutorial, make sure that you have the following "
"packages installed:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "IBM Quantum Compute client (`pip install qiskit-ibm-runtime`)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Qiskit Functions (`pip install qiskit-ibm-catalog`)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "scikit-learn (`pip install scikit-learn`)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "pandas (`pip install pandas`)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Matplotlib (`pip install matplotlib`)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"You also need access to the Rimay function from the [Qiskit Functions "
"Catalog](https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/functions).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Setup {#setup}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Import the packages and define two helpers: one that trains a random forest,"
" and one that computes the metrics used throughout the tutorial.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Configure IBM Quantum credentials {#configure-ibm-quantum-credentials}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Authenticate with your [IBM Quantum Platform API "
"key](https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/), load the Rimay function, and select "
"the least busy quantum processor available to your instance.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 1: Map classical inputs to a quantum problem {#step-1-map-classical-"
"inputs-to-a-quantum-problem}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Rimay maps every numerical input feature onto one qubit, so the first step "
"is to turn the raw table into numerical features.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Load the dataset {#load-the-dataset}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Load the German credit dataset directly from OpenML. The `class` column "
"holds the label: `good` for applicants who repaid their credit and `bad` for"
" those who did not.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Encode the features {#encode-the-features}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Convert the label to a binary value, and one-hot encode the 13 categorical "
"columns. The code drops the first category of each column to avoid redundant"
" features, which results in 48 numerical features.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Split into training and test sets {#split-into-training-and-test-sets}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Hold back 20% of the applicants as a test set. A stratified split keeps the "
"share of good and bad credits equal in both sets.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Establish a classical baseline {#establish-a-classical-baseline}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Train a random forest on the classical features. In Step 4, you compare the "
"quantum-transformed features against its metrics.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Upload the data to Rimay {#upload-the-data-to-rimay}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Rimay reads its inputs from CSV files that you upload to the function. The "
"features and the labels go into separate files. You also upload the test "
"set, so that Rimay maps it with the same mapping it fits on the training "
"set.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 2: Optimize the problem for quantum hardware execution "
"{#step-2-optimize-the-problem-for-quantum-hardware-execution}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Rimay builds and transpiles the circuits for the processor you select, so "
"there is no circuit to optimize by hand. Specify the number of shots per "
"circuit, within the following constraints:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Every feature needs one qubit, so the processor must have at least as many "
"qubits as the dataset has features.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Rimay runs one circuit per row, training and test rows together. A single "
"job can request at most 10 million executions, so the number of rows times "
"`shots` must stay within that budget.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Check both limits before you submit the job.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition type=\"note\">\n"
"A job must also finish within 24 hours, including the time its circuits wait in the queue, or it is stopped without a result.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Calculate the function size {#calculate-the-function-size}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Every job runs at a function size, `S`, `M`, or `L`, which selects the "
"compute resources it runs on. For Rimay, the size depends on the number of "
"rows that a job maps, training and test rows together. If you request a size"
" that is too small, the job fails with error `4600`. For the sizes and their"
" limits, see [Function sizes](/guides/kipu-rimay#function-sizes) in the "
"Rimay guide.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The following cell defines the size calculator. It runs on your machine, "
"reads only the data that you pass to it, and submits nothing.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Pass the data sets in the same order as the files in `input_file`, together "
"with the mode of the run:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 3: Execute using Qiskit primitives {#step-3-execute-using-qiskit-"
"primitives}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Run Rimay in `fit-transform` mode. It fits a quantum feature mapping on "
"`x_train.csv` and `y_train.csv`, and maps both the training and the test "
"sets with it. The order of the files in `input_file` matters. List the "
"training features, the training labels, and then the optional test features."
" Pass the size that you calculated as `function_size`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Monitor the job {#monitor-the-job}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Check the status of the job while it runs. `job.result()` blocks until the "
"job has finished.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The result holds a `mapping_id`, which identifies the mapping that this run "
"fitted. You do not need it in this tutorial, because the test set was mapped"
" in the same run. Record this ID if you want to map new rows later with "
"`mode=\"transform\"`. It is the only way to access the fitted mapping, and "
"without it, you must fit and pay for a new one. The mapping is built for the"
" qubit layout of this processor, so a later `transform` run must pass the "
"same `backend_name`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 4: Post-process and return the result in the desired classical format "
"{#step-4-post-process-and-return-the-result-in-the-desired-classical-format}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Download the quantum-transformed features {#download-the-quantum-"
"transformed-features}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Download the files that the job wrote, by using the names in `output_files`."
" Each name starts with the job ID, so the files of this run stay available "
"after later runs. Pass `target_name` so that each file is saved under its "
"own name.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Train on the quantum-transformed features {#train-on-the-quantum-"
"transformed-features}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Train a random forest on the quantum-transformed features alone.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Train on classical and quantum features combined {#train-on-classical-and-"
"quantum-features-combined}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Stack the original and the quantum-transformed features, and train the same "
"model on the combination.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Compare the results {#compare-the-results}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
